---
description: AHPRA / TGA / advertising locks for Apex
alwaysApply: true
---

Apex is an Australian doctor-led telehealth clinic. Advertising law applies to every string.

Never:

- Name a medication, compound, peptide, hormone product, or dose. "Peptide consultation" is already on the site; do not add more of that language. Prefer "doctor-prescribed protocol" / "clinical protocol".
- Imply a result. No before/after. No "you'll lose X". No guaranteed eligibility or approval.
- Diagnose, prescribe, or give personal medical advice. The doctor does that after assessment.
- Quote a price in the chatbot. Point to `/pricing` or the portal.
- Treat AHPRA registration as a stat. It is a sentence.

Always:

- Suitability and prescribing stay with an AHPRA-registered doctor.
- Pathology is arranged at accredited collection centres. Results inform the consult. They do not approve treatment.
- Urgent symptoms → call 000.
- Email copy follows the rules at the top of `lib/email/templates.ts` (service vs marketing, no approval language).

If a requested line would not survive a TGA advertising review, do not write it. Say why in one sentence.
